Jukka Lahtinen wrote:
 > David Damerell <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> writes:

 >> Tracking start time would also prevent the
 >> approach of building streaks by selectively
 >> finishing games across multiple servers.

 > But does it NEED to be prevented?

Probably yes.

The implicit contract in "longest streak" is that
those are games started, and played, one after
another [with no intervening games started but
abandoned so as to go unnoticed as games
interrupting the streak].

Catering for abandoning games one turn short of
defeat (or just "that aren't going well") without
bring them to completion, so that games started
after that one can be used to extend the streak
of games started before that one, smacks heavily of
cheating with respect to that implicit contract.

Redefining "streak" to mean "streak of games in
order _by start times_ with *all start times
considered across all servers even for unascended or
uncompleted games*" would quash that mode of
cheating, I think.

Doing that would return the meaning of the prize to
the originally intended one: the prize goes to the
person who can play the most _consecutive_ games one
after another while successfully ascending each one.

